What is instruction reordering?
Instruction reordering means the compiler or CPU changes the order in which instructions are executed, while preserving the behavior required by the language and hardware rules.
Why? Performance optimization. The compiler and CPU try to execute instructions efficiently instead of always following the exact source-code order.
Consider:
int a = 0;
int b = 0;

a = 10;  // Statement 1
b = 20;  // Statement 2
The compiler or CPU may arrange these independent operations differently if doing so is safe under the applicable rules.
Rule: as-if-serial
Reordering tab tak allowed hai jab tak single thread ka result same rahe. Ye rule multi-thread ko kuch guarantee nahi karta, yahi problem ki jad hai.

Praticale Bug
int data = 0;
boolean ready = false;   // volatile nahi hai

// Thread 1                 // Thread 2
data = 42;                  while (!ready) { }
ready = true;               System.out.println(data);  // 0 aa sakta hai!

Compiler aur CPU order badal sakte hain jab tak single thread ko fark na pade. Multi-thread mein wahi bug ban jaata hai. 

Three important concepts

Compiler reordering
The compiler optimizes the generated machine instructions.

CPU reordering
The processor may execute or complete independent operations in a different order internally.

Visibility
One thread may not immediately observe another thread's writes without the required synchronization.
4. How do we control it?
Java provides mechanisms such as:
	•	volatile  
	•	synchronized  
	•	Locks and other concurrency utilities  
These establish specific ordering and visibility guarantees. At the hardware level, JVMs may use memory barriers or fences when necessary.
int a = 0;
int b = 0;

a = 10;
b = a + 5;

Here, b depends on the value written to a. An optimization cannot simply execute b = a + 5 first if that would change the program's required single-threaded behavior.
This gives us the first rule:
An optimization must preserve the required program behavior. Dependencies constrain reordering.
Multithreading introduces a further complication: the Java Memory Model defines which observations are legal when multiple threads access shared state.

